## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests privacy-first, scalable analytics across regions with data contracts.

## Key Concepts

- Per-region pseudonymization
- Iceberg upserts
- Event-time windows and lateness handling
- Immutable audits and data contracts
- Spark vs Flink trade-offs for streaming

## Code Example

```javascript
// Region-aware salt hash (Node.js)
const crypto = require('crypto');
function saltedHash(region, id, salts){
  const salt = salts[region] ?? salts.default;
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(id + salt).digest('hex');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency and audit coverage?
- How would you handle a new region with a different privacy policy?
